==Spanish==

===Etymology===
Probably borrowed from {{bor|es|it|pigliare}} or {{bor|es|fr|piller}}. Compare also {{cog|pt|pilhar}} and English {{m|en|pillage}}. {{doublet|es|pelar}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{es-IPA}}
* {{audio|es|LL-Q1321 (spa)-AdrianAbdulBaha-pillar.wav|Audio (Colombia)}}

===Verb===
{{es-verb}}

# to [[catch]], [[get]], to [[grab]] {{gloss|e.g. grab a cab, get lunch, grab a drink, catch a movie}}
# to [[pilfer]], [[steal]]
# {{lb|es|game}} to [[tag]]
# {{lb|es|colloquial}} to [[get]] (a joke)
# {{lb|es|colloquial}} to [[catch]], to [[catch up]] to
# {{lb|es|colloquial}} to [[catch]], to [[pick up]], to [[bust]], to [[nab]] (someone doing something illegal)
#: {{syn|es|atrapar|sorprender}}
# {{lb|es|colloquial}} to [[come down with]], [[catch]], to [[pick up]] (an illness)
# {{lb|es|colloquial}} to [[pick up on]], to [[take]] {{gloss|e.g. information, a hint}}
# {{lb|es|Spain|colloquial}} to [[score]] {{gloss|e.g. drugs}}
# {{lb|es|colloquial|reflexive}} to [[jam]] (your finger)
#: {{uxi|es|Me '''pillé''' el dedo con la puerta.|I jammed my finger in the door.}}
# {{lb|es|colloquial|reflexive}} to [[fall in love]], to [[crush on]] someone
#: {{uxi|es|Creo que se ha '''pillado''' de mí.|I think she may have a crush on me.}}

